The generated semantics counts UTF-8 bytes instead of Python Unicode characters. For valid input `["😀", "abc"]`, Python returns `"abc"`, while fresh K execution returns `"😀"` with count 4. The internal proof reconstructs and is non-vacuous, but proves correctness only for the incorrect byte-oriented model.

